  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 230 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nThe synoptic-scale pattern will change across the continental \nUnited States as a longwave trough develops across the eastern \nhalf of the country. This pattern will allow a cold front to \nadvance southward as the trough amplifies to the east. Currently, \nthe cold front is most likely to move through on Wednesday. \nHowever, the cold air may lag behind the initial wind shift with \nthe coolest air arriving for Thursday and Friday. Rain chances \nalso look to be near zero/limited with the front with the more \nappreciable synoptic-scale forcing remaining to the northeast.\n\nHow cold will the air mass be behind the front? The Southern \nPlains will be to the southwest of the strongest cold air \nadvection, which means the chance of a significant cool-down (for \nNovember) is very low. Bias-corrected probabilistic guidance \nindicates a ~20 deg F spread between the 10th and 90th \npercentiles, with the cooler end of ensemble guidance only \nlowering high temperatures into the 50s deg F (which is slightly \nbelow normal for mid-November). Currently, The most likely \nscenario is highs in the low 60s (northeast) to the low 70s deg F \n(southwest). \n\nMahale",
